{"id": "mfab-Q18225002", "status": "ok", "meta": "An oval portrait of a young girl in a gold-trimmed cloak, signed and dated by Rembrandt in 1632, and its long trail through private collections.", "about": "An oval portrait of a girl with curly golden hair, drop earrings and a dark cloak edged with gold lace. She looks straight at the viewer. Rembrandt signed and dated the work in 1632. Late in the 19th century Prince Johann II of Liechtenstein bought it and passed it on to a bishop of Chur. The bishop sold it to a Boston dealer in 1929. The collector Robert Treat Paine II then bought it and lent it to the Museum of Fine Arts. His son inherited it, and the family kept it until a sale at Sotheby's in London in 1986.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AYoung_Girl_in_a_Gold-Trimmed_Cloak%2C_Rembrandt_van_Rijn%2C_1632%2C_The_Leiden_Collection%2C_New_York_City.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AYoung_Girl_in_a_Gold-Trimmed_Cloak%2C_Rembrandt_van_Rijn%2C_1632%2C_The_Leiden_Collection%2C_New_York_City.jpg", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_a_Young_Woman_(Rembrandt)"], "check": "clean"}
