{"id": "mfab-Q20635123", "status": "ok", "meta": "Whistler's night view of the Venetian lagoon, painted around 1880 during his only stay in Venice and now in the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston.", "about": "Night over the Venetian lagoon, seen from the Royal Gardens toward San Giorgio and the Lido. Whistler painted it during his only visit to Venice, which lasted fifteen months. The Fine Art Society had sent him there to make etchings after his libel case against John Ruskin left him short of money. He painted just two oils in the city. This one was never shown in his lifetime. Wikipedia says his model Carmen Rossi allegedly took it from the studio and owned it for a while. The Museum of Fine Arts bought it in 1942. Look for the tall ship at the left and the bell tower at the right.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AJames_Abbott_McNeill_Whistler_-_Nocturne_in_Blue_and_Silver-_The_Lagoon%2C_Venice_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AJames_Abbott_McNeill_Whistler_-_Nocturne_in_Blue_and_Silver-_The_Lagoon%2C_Venice_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Lagoon,_Venice"], "check": "clean"}
